; P11 M0 -- how an Asteroids frame should erase itself, measured before any
; game code is written.  See docs/asteroids-design.md section 3 and section 16.
;
;   erall                     <-- REQUIRED: see below
;   load "p11rocks
;   p11rocks
;
; Asteroids is the first game in this tree that is not a sprite game: its
; rocks are hollow rotating outlines, which is turtle graphics.  The one
; question that shape raises, and the whole of the design's risk, is how last
; frame's lines get taken away.  Two candidates, and this script builds
; neither game and times both on a static scene of 6, 9 and 12 rocks:
;
;   erase in place  -- draw every shape again in the background colour from
;                      the state that drew it, then draw it at its new place.
;                      Two drawing passes, but the present touches only the
;                      tiles the strokes covered.
;   clear + redraw  -- wipe the canvas and re-trace the display list.  One
;                      drawing pass, but the present is the whole screen.
;
; Until B16 was fixed (2026-08-11) the second one was not a candidate at all:
; `clean` wrote through to the panel even in manual refresh mode, so the
; screen visibly blanked every frame.  With the wipe deferred to the present
; it is a memset, and the comparison is a real one -- which is why this
; script exists and why it runs before M1.
;
; Six things it measures, all of them to a FILE:
;
;   1-2. body and present, separately, for each strategy at 6, 9 and 12 rocks
;   3.   the present a 12-rock dirty region actually costs, against the
;        full-screen present the clear strategy pays -- the number that
;        decides section 3
;   4.   a drawing statement (`fd 17`), which section 12 assumes at 35 us and
;        nobody has measured; it is the number the whole frame budget rests on
;   5.   an arithmetic statement and a bare `repeat`, the two brackets P10
;        measured, on this board in this run so the comparison is within one
;        machine
;   6.   the shape dispatch, which section 12's table does not count and which
;        the erase strategy pays twice a frame per rock
;
; Four things about how it measures, each of which took a bug to learn:
;
;   `fullscreen`, not the prompt's text mode.  `screen_gfx_blit_dirty` returns
;   immediately in text mode, so a present measured there is zero and the
;   frame figure is a Logo body with no present in it -- the correction P9 had
;   to make to its entire first series of numbers.
;
;   `refresh`, not `sync`.  The number wanted is what the work costs, not the
;   cadence; `sync` would wait out the rest of the frame and report it.
;
;   `clean`, not `cs`.  A full reset also restores automatic refresh
;   (`prim_clearscreen`, core/primitives_turtle.c), which would silently end
;   the manual-mode measurement half way through it.
;
;   The scene does not move.  In the game a rock steps a couple of pixels and
;   rotates between the erase and the draw, so the two passes cover slightly
;   different pixels -- but both stay inside the same bounding box, and the
;   dirty tracker works in 16-pixel tiles, so the dirty region is the same to
;   within a tile.  Holding the scene still keeps the body figure free of
;   physics that M1 will measure properly.
;
; THE ERASE IS A PEN COLOUR, NOT `pe`.  The design's section 3.2 wrote it as
; `pe place :i draw.rock :i`, and that cannot work: pen up/down/erase/reverse
; are one enum (`LogoPen`, devices/console.h), and every outline procedure
; contains a `pd` in its prologue -- so the shape's own `pd` would cancel the
; `pe` before a single segment was drawn.  Drawing in the background colour is
; what the tree already does for HUD text, it needs the canvas to be otherwise
; empty in exactly the same way, and it is cheaper: two statements a frame
; instead of two per rock per pass.
;
; `erall` is not optional.  MAX_PROCEDURES is a hard 128 and this file defines
; 24; a workspace still holding a game can run out of procedure slots, and
; `load` then stops with `out of space` -- the procedure table talking, not
; memory.
;
; The report goes to `p11rocks.txt` as well as the screen, because numbers on
; the PicoCalc's display cannot be copied anywhere.  Runs append, so a session
; accumulates in one file; note beside each which board it was.

make "p11.frames 60      ; frames averaged per point
make "p11.calib 2000     ; iterations per calibration loop

make "p11.fg 254         ; white, as the arcade rocks are
make "p11.bg 255         ; the current background slot -- this is the eraser

;==========================================================================
; The scene
;
; Twelve rocks on a fixed lattice, ordered so that the first 6 and the first 9
; are each spread over the field and each hold an equal number of large,
; medium and small.  Rocks 6 and 10 straddle an edge, which is the case
; section 4 costs separately: a straddling rock dirties tiles at both ends of
; its tile rows and a row's span is inclusive, so it pays its full rows.
; Nothing overlaps, so a reading is not confounded by the one-pixel hole an
; erase punches in a crossing outline.

make "p11.x  [-105   95  -85   80    0 -155  -30   45 -120  135   25  110]
make "p11.y  [  95   85  -80  -95   10    0  120  -25 -140  120  152  -10]
make "p11.sz [   3    2    1    3    2    1    3    2    1    3    2    1]
make "p11.sh [   1    2    3    2    3    1    3    1    2    2    3    1]
make "p11.a  [   0   40   80  120  160  200  240  280  320   20   60  100]

; Where the answers land, so the report is one list of lines rather than two
; that can drift apart.  Built with `list`, not as literals: a literal belongs
; to the procedure's stored body and `.setitem` would scribble on it for good.
make "p11.ib (list 0 0 0)    ; erase in place -- body, ms
make "p11.ip (list 0 0 0)    ; erase in place -- present, ms
make "p11.cb (list 0 0 0)    ; clear + redraw -- body, ms
make "p11.cp (list 0 0 0)    ; clear + redraw -- present, ms

;==========================================================================
; The outlines
;
; Generated by scripts/gen_rocks.py -- do not hand-edit.  Each is nothing but
; `fd`/`rt` with literal arguments: on this board an arithmetic statement
; costs 48 us and a literal-argument primitive call is a fraction of that, so
; computing a vertex would cost more than drawing it.  The scale is baked into
; the literals, three sizes per outline, rather than multiplied by a radius.
;
; `pu fd <r> rt <t> pd` walks from the rock's centre out to its first vertex,
; which is what lets the stored position mean the centre while the walk starts
; at a corner.  There is no turn after the last segment -- `place` sets the
; heading before every pass, so that turn would never be read.

to rock.a.l
  pu fd 21.1  rt 112.8  pd
  fd 16.1  rt 47.5  fd 15.8  rt 48.8  fd 14.8  rt 32.1  fd 14.8  rt 56.1
  fd 14.9  rt 47.3  fd 13.8  rt 28.5  fd 14.1  rt 48.5  fd 15.6
end

to rock.a.m
  pu fd 12.4  rt 122.8  pd
  fd 12.1  rt 56  fd 11.8  rt 58.5  fd 12.3  rt 58.2  fd 13.3  rt 75
  fd 12.7  rt 44.4  fd 11.8
end

to rock.a.s
  pu fd 7.6  rt 131.7  pd
  fd 8.4  rt 60.5  fd 8.4  rt 83.1  fd 8.4  rt 61.3  fd 8.5  rt 77.6
  fd 9
end

to rock.b.l
  pu fd 19.4  rt 120  pd
  fd 14.2  rt 26.5  fd 14.7  rt 67.7  fd 14.7  rt 18.2  fd 15.3  rt 66.1
  fd 15.8  rt 44.2  fd 14.6  rt 38.4  fd 14.1  rt 43.6  fd 14.5
end

to rock.b.m
  pu fd 11.5  rt 120.9  pd
  fd 11.4  rt 57  fd 11.6  rt 63.3  fd 11.6  rt 54.9  fd 12  rt 66
  fd 12.2  rt 59.6  fd 11.7
end

to rock.b.s
  pu fd 6.9  rt 129.1  pd
  fd 7.9  rt 65.8  fd 7.9  rt 79.7  fd 7.7  rt 60  fd 8.1  rt 82.1
  fd 8.5
end

to rock.c.l
  pu fd 17.5  rt 104.1  pd
  fd 14.4  rt 66.3  fd 14.2  rt 28.9  fd 12.8  rt 50.6  fd 12.9  rt 38.2
  fd 13.1  rt 43.9  fd 14.1  rt 51.8  fd 14.4  rt 48.3  fd 13.9
end

to rock.c.m
  pu fd 12.7  rt 128  pd
  fd 11.8  rt 40.8  fd 12.4  rt 69.9  fd 13.7  rt 64.9  fd 13.4  rt 63.6
  fd 12.1  rt 46.6  fd 12
end

to rock.c.s
  pu fd 7.8  rt 128.7  pd
  fd 8.9  rt 66.1  fd 9  rt 77.5  fd 9.1  rt 74.2  fd 8.4  rt 61.3
  fd 8.6
end

;==========================================================================
; Drawing a rock
;
; `seth` before a turtle-relative walk rotates the whole polygon about the
; stored centre, so a rotating rock costs exactly what a still one costs.
; `setx`/`sety` rather than `setpos`, which takes a list and would allocate
; one per rock per pass.

to place :i
  pu  setx item :i :p11.x  sety item :i :p11.y  seth item :i :p11.a
end

; The literal goes on the LEFT of every comparison here.  A call's last
; argument absorbs trailing infix, so `item :i :p11.sz = 3` would ask for
; item :i of (:p11.sz = 3); the alternative spelling is `(item ...) = 3`, and
; the grouping paren costs 11 % of an expression for nothing.
to draw.rock :i
  if 3 = item :i :p11.sz [draw.large :i stop]
  if 2 = item :i :p11.sz [draw.medium :i stop]
  draw.small :i
end

to draw.large :i
  if 1 = item :i :p11.sh [rock.a.l stop]
  if 2 = item :i :p11.sh [rock.b.l stop]
  rock.c.l
end

to draw.medium :i
  if 1 = item :i :p11.sh [rock.a.m stop]
  if 2 = item :i :p11.sh [rock.b.m stop]
  rock.c.m
end

to draw.small :i
  if 1 = item :i :p11.sh [rock.a.s stop]
  if 2 = item :i :p11.sh [rock.b.s stop]
  rock.c.s
end

to pass :n
  repeat :n [place repcount  draw.rock repcount]
end

;==========================================================================
; The two frames

to frame.inplace :n
  setpc :p11.bg  pass :n
  setpc :p11.fg  pass :n
end

to frame.clear :n
  clean
  setpc :p11.fg  pass :n
end

;==========================================================================
; Timing
;
; Body and present are read apart, which is what P9 M5 wished it had: a frame
; that misses its budget is a different problem depending on which half is
; over.  The two answers come back in globals because a procedure outputs one
; value and both are wanted.

to time.inplace :n
  local "t0
  make "p11.body 0
  make "p11.pres 0
  repeat :p11.frames [
    make "t0 ticks
    frame.inplace :n
    make "p11.body :p11.body + (ticks - :t0)
    make "t0 ticks
    refresh
    make "p11.pres :p11.pres + (ticks - :t0)
  ]
  make "p11.body :p11.body / :p11.frames
  make "p11.pres :p11.pres / :p11.frames
end

to time.clear :n
  local "t0
  make "p11.body 0
  make "p11.pres 0
  repeat :p11.frames [
    make "t0 ticks
    frame.clear :n
    make "p11.body :p11.body + (ticks - :t0)
    make "t0 ticks
    refresh
    make "p11.pres :p11.pres + (ticks - :t0)
  ]
  make "p11.body :p11.body / :p11.frames
  make "p11.pres :p11.pres / :p11.frames
end

to measure :k :n
  clean
  refresh
  setpc :p11.fg
  pass :n            ; the scene has to be on the canvas before an erase can
  refresh            ; take it away, or the first frame erases nothing
  time.inplace :n
  .setitem :k :p11.ib :p11.body
  .setitem :k :p11.ip :p11.pres
  time.clear :n
  .setitem :k :p11.cb :p11.body
  .setitem :k :p11.cp :p11.pres
end

;==========================================================================
; Calibration
;
; Run last, because the first of these scribbles a rosette over the whole
; canvas and every present after it would be a full screen.

; Microseconds per drawing statement -- the number section 12 assumes at 35 us
; and leans on hardest.  `fd 17 rt 41` with the pen down is exactly the shape
; the outlines are made of, so the pair is timed and halved.  Some of these
; strokes cross an edge and are split by `wrap`, as a rock's are.
to calib.draw
  local "t0
  pd
  make "t0 ticks
  repeat :p11.calib [fd 17 rt 41]
  output (ticks - :t0) * 1000 / (:p11.calib * 2)
end

; The upper bracket: an arithmetic statement, P10 M5's 48 us.
to calib.arith
  local "t0 local "x
  make "x 0
  make "t0 ticks
  repeat :p11.calib [make "x :x + 1]
  output (ticks - :t0) * 1000 / :p11.calib
end

; The floor: a `repeat` iteration with nothing in it, so the two lines above
; can be read net of the loop that carried them.
to calib.bare
  local "t0
  make "t0 ticks
  repeat :p11.calib []
  output (ticks - :t0) * 1000 / :p11.calib
end

; Microseconds the 9-way shape dispatch adds to one rock, one pass -- a
; procedure call plus two `if` chains, which section 12's table does not
; count and the erase strategy pays 24 times a frame.  Rock 1 is large
; outline A, so the second loop is the same drawing with the dispatch
; taken out.
to calib.dispatch
  local "t0 local "with local "without
  make "t0 ticks
  repeat :p11.calib [place 1  draw.rock 1]
  make "with ticks - :t0
  make "t0 ticks
  repeat :p11.calib [place 1  rock.a.l]
  make "without ticks - :t0
  output (:with - :without) * 1000 / :p11.calib
end

;==========================================================================
; The report

; Every punctuation mark in a printed list is tokenised as a word of its own,
; so `clear+redraw` comes out as `clear + redraw` and `--` as `- -`.  The
; report therefore spells everything in words.

to line.for :k :n
  pr (se :n [rocks])
  pr (se [  in place    body] item :k :p11.ib [ms   present] item :k :p11.ip [ms])
  pr (se [  clear redraw body] item :k :p11.cb [ms   present] item :k :p11.cp [ms])
  pr (se [  frame       in place] (item :k :p11.ib) + (item :k :p11.ip) [ms   clear redraw] (item :k :p11.cb) + (item :k :p11.cp) [ms])
  pr []
end

to p11.report
  pr [P11 M0 how an Asteroids frame gets erased]
  pr (se [frames per point] :p11.frames)
  pr []
  line.for 1 6
  line.for 2 9
  line.for 3 12
  pr [calibration, microseconds]
  pr (se [  drawing statement    ] :p11.stmt)
  pr (se [  arithmetic statement ] :p11.arith)
  pr (se [  bare repeat iteration] :p11.bare)
  pr (se [  shape dispatch       ] :p11.disp)
  pr []
  pr [the decision at 12 rocks]
  pr (se [  dirty present] item 3 :p11.ip [ms of a full screen at] item 3 :p11.cp [ms])
  pr [  budget at 20 fps is 50 ms]
  pr []
end

to p11rocks
  fullscreen
  setrefresh "manual
  clean
  refresh
  measure 1 6
  measure 2 9
  measure 3 12
  make "p11.stmt calib.draw
  make "p11.arith calib.arith
  make "p11.bare calib.bare
  make "p11.disp calib.dispatch
  clean
  refresh
  setrefresh "auto
  textscreen
  open "p11rocks.txt
  setwrite "p11rocks.txt
  p11.report
  setwrite []
  close "p11rocks.txt
  p11.report
  pr [Also written to p11rocks.txt]
end
